{
  "requirement": "Distinguishes a domain operation failure, a missing agent, a provider connection or request failure, an agent protocol failure, and an error encoding or decoding the output.\n\n```rust,ignore\npub enum ValidationError<E> {\n    Graph(E),\n    NoAgentConfigured,\n    Agent(String),\n    Provider(std::io::Error),\n    Output(serde_json::Error),\n}\n```\n\nTrait implementations\n\n```rust,ignore\nimpl<E: Debug> Debug for ValidationError<E>;\nimpl<E: Display> Display for ValidationError<E>;\nimpl<E: Error + 'static> Error for ValidationError<E>;\n```",
  "determination": false,
  "rationale": "**Confirmation:** the assessments disagreed\n\nFirst assessment `true`: structural: The text prescribes a public type and trait impls: the generic enum `ValidationError<E>` with variants `Graph(E)`, `NoAgentConfigured`, `Agent(String)`, `Provider(std::io::Error)` and `Output(serde_json::Error)`, plus `Debug` (E: Debug), `Display` (E: Display) and `Error` (E: Error + 'static). Alternatives such as a struct with a kind field, or different payload types, would violate these clauses. The roots cover each one in `crates/trust-but-verify/src/graph.rs`. `enum[ValidationError]` declares exactly those variants and payload types and derives `Debug`, which gives the bound `E: Debug`. `impl[ValidationError<E>/std::fmt::Display]` has the bound `E: std::fmt::Display` and defines `fmt`. `impl[ValidationError<E>/std::error::Error]` has the bound `E: std::error::Error + 'static` and defines `source`. All three roots carry {impl} markers, and no other structural obligation is left uncovered.\n\nbehavioral: The requirement fixes five variants with distinct meanings, plus Debug, Display and Error implementations. The reviewed tests exercise each variant, and the assertions distinguish them.\n\n- **`Graph(E)`**: `crates/trust-but-verify/tests/contracts.rs#fn[refresh_propagates_load_decode_check_execution_and_save_errors]` checks that load, read, edges, execute and store failures come back as `ValidationError::Graph` with the original value. It also checks that the saved record is unchanged. `validation_tests.rs#fn[agent_errors_expose_the_agreed_string_payload]` checks that `source()` on `Graph` returns the inner error.\n- **`NoAgentConfigured`**: `agent/tests.rs#fn[deterministic_validation_uses_required_storage_without_an_agent]` and `graph/validation_tests.rs#fn[runner_does_not_store_a_missing_agent_error_and_supports_intermediate_calls]` check that asking without an agent returns this variant. The second test also checks that nothing is stored.\n- **`Agent(String)`**: `agent_errors_expose_the_agreed_string_payload` builds the variant and checks that the payload is a `String`, that `to_string()` is \"agent: provider detail\", and that `source()` is `None`. `agent/tests.rs#fn[assistant_text_and_provider_failure_cannot_become_typed_results]` and `agent/tests.rs#fn[zero_and_multiple_submissions_share_one_reminder_and_a_second_violation_ends]` also assert `Agent` for protocol failures. I saw both in search results and did not read them in full. `validation_tests.rs#fn[ending_without_a_corrected_submission_is_an_agent_error]` asserts `Agent` too. Its search snippet carried no {test} marker for this requirement, and I did not read the full function.\n- **`Provider(io::Error)`**: `agent/configured.rs#mod[tests]/fn[configured_agents_initialize_only_when_asked_and_report_setup_errors]` checks that a missing credential gives `Provider(_)`. It also checks that the message names the environment variable, which exercises `Display`.\n- **`Output(serde_json::Error)`**: `contracts.rs#fn[refresh_serialization_failure_preserves_the_saved_record]` and `contracts.rs#fn[serialization_failure_leaves_the_existing_record_unchanged]` check that an encode failure gives `Output`. `serialization_failure_leaves_the_existing_record_unchanged` also checks that `Display` carries the message. The \"decode\" stages in `refresh_propagates_load_decode_check_execution_and_save_errors` check that a decode failure gives `Output`.\n- **Trait implementations**: `Debug` is used in the panic formatting. `Display` and `Error::source` are checked above. The requirement gives no specific text or source behaviour beyond these.\n\nThe public-export test `tests/public_api.rs#fn[public_exports_match_the_appendix]` covers the declared shape structurally. I found no obligation without a test, and no acknowledgments apply.\n\nSecond assessment `false`: Add `{test}` to `crates/trust-but-verify/src/graph/validation_tests.rs#fn[agent_errors_expose_the_agreed_string_payload]` — The marked tests cover Graph (`refresh_propagates_load_decode_check_execution_and_save_errors`), NoAgentConfigured (`deterministic_validation_uses_required_storage_without_an_agent` and `runner_does_not_store_a_missing_agent_error_and_supports_intermediate_calls`), Provider (`configured_agents_initialize_only_when_asked_and_report_setup_errors`) and Output (`refresh_serialization_failure_preserves_the_saved_record` and `serialization_failure_leaves_the_existing_record_unchanged`). None of them covers the `Agent(String)` variant, which is the agent protocol failure. This test checks that `Agent` carries a String, that its Display is `agent: provider detail`, and that its `Error::source` is None. It also checks that `Graph` exposes its source through `Error::source`. It exercises the Display and Error trait implementations, so it needs a {test} reference to `requirements.sdk.graph.running-criteria.ValidationError`.\n\nAdd `{test}` to `crates/trust-but-verify/src/graph/validation_tests.rs#fn[ending_without_a_corrected_submission_is_an_agent_error]` — This test shows a real agent protocol failure, an agent ending without a corrected submission, surfacing as `ValidationError::Agent(_)`. It does this through `ask` and through `verify_with_agent`, and it shows that no record is stored. It supplies behavioural evidence for the `Agent` variant, which is distinct from the other variants, and it has no {test} reference to this requirement.\n\nRecorded the second assessment (`false`).",
